var e={level:`B2`,type:`error-correct`,questions:[{id:`b2-ec-001`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Do not use 'would' in the if-clause. Use the past simple: if I had.`,wrongReasons:{"if i would have more time, i would learn spanish":`'would' cannot be used in the if-clause of a conditional. Use the past simple: if I had.`,"if i have more time, i would learn spanish":`'would learn' needs the past simple in the if-clause (second conditional): if I had.`},wrong:`If I would have more time, I would learn Spanish.`,solutions:[`If I had more time, I would learn Spanish.`]},{id:`b2-ec-002`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Suggest' is not followed by an object + to-infinitive. Use 'suggested that I take' or 'suggested taking'.`,wrongReasons:{"she suggested me to take a taxi":`'suggest' does not take an object + to-infinitive (unlike 'advise' or 'tell'). Use 'suggested that I take' or 'suggested taking'.`},wrong:`She suggested me to take a taxi.`,solutions:[`She suggested that I take a taxi.`,`She suggested that I should take a taxi.`,`She suggested taking a taxi.`,`She suggested I take a taxi.`]},{id:`b2-ec-003`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Third conditional (past situations): if + past perfect, would have + past participle. Example: If I had seen him, I would have said hello.`,wrongReasons:{"if she had left earlier, she would caught the train":`After 'would' you need 'have' + past participle: 'would have caught'.`,"if she had left earlier, she will have caught the train":`'will have' is not used in the result clause of a third conditional. Use 'would have caught'.`},wrong:`If she had left earlier, she would catch the train.`,solutions:[`If she had left earlier, she would have caught the train.`,`If she had left earlier she would have caught the train.`,`If she had left earlier, she'd have caught the train.`,`If she had left earlier she'd have caught the train.`]},{id:`b2-ec-004`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Unless' already means 'if not', so the verb after it is positive. Example: Unless you hurry, you'll be late.`,wrongReasons:{"unless you don't book in advance, you will not get a table":`This still has a double negative: 'unless' + 'don't'. Remove 'don't'.`,"unless you booked in advance, you won't get a table":`This is a first conditional about the future, so use the present simple after 'unless': 'unless you book'.`},wrong:`Unless you don't book in advance, you won't get a table.`,solutions:[`Unless you book in advance, you won't get a table.`,`Unless you book in advance you will not get a table.`,`Unless you book in advance you won't get a table.`,`Unless you book in advance, you will not get a table.`,`If you don't book in advance, you won't get a table.`,`If you do not book in advance you will not get a table.`,`If you do not book in advance you won't get a table.`,`If you do not book in advance, you will not get a table.`,`If you do not book in advance, you won't get a table.`,`If you don't book in advance you will not get a table.`,`If you don't book in advance you won't get a table.`,`If you don't book in advance, you will not get a table.`]},{id:`b2-ec-005`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`In a first conditional, use the present simple after 'if', even for the future. Example: If it rains tomorrow, we'll stay at home.`,wrongReasons:{"if the weather would be fine tomorrow, we'll have the picnic in the park":`'would' cannot follow 'if' here. Use the present simple: 'if the weather is fine'.`,"if the weather was fine tomorrow, we'll have the picnic in the park":`Do not mix a past form with 'will'. For a real future possibility, use 'is': 'if the weather is fine'.`},wrong:`If the weather will be fine tomorrow, we'll have the picnic in the park.`,solutions:[`If the weather is fine tomorrow, we'll have the picnic in the park.`,`If the weather is fine tomorrow we will have the picnic in the park.`,`If the weather is fine tomorrow we'll have the picnic in the park.`,`If the weather is fine tomorrow, we will have the picnic in the park.`]},{id:`b2-ec-006`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Mixed conditional: a past condition (if + past perfect) with a present result (would + verb). Example: If I had saved money, I would be rich now.`,wrongReasons:{"if i took that job in berlin last year, i would have lived in germany now":`The condition is still wrong: 'last year' needs the past perfect 'had taken'. The result is about now, so 'would live' is correct.`,"if i had took that job in berlin last year, i would live in germany now":`The past participle of 'take' is 'taken', not 'took': 'had taken'.`},wrong:`If I took that job in Berlin last year, I would live in Germany now.`,solutions:[`If I had taken that job in Berlin last year, I would live in Germany now.`,`If I had taken that job in Berlin last year I would live in Germany now.`,`If I had taken that job in Berlin last year, I'd live in Germany now.`,`If I had taken that job in Berlin last year I'd live in Germany now.`,`If I had taken that job in Berlin last year, I would be living in Germany now.`,`If I had taken that job in Berlin last year I would be living in Germany now.`,`If I had taken that job in Berlin last year, I'd be living in Germany now.`,`If I had taken that job in Berlin last year I'd be living in Germany now.`]},{id:`b2-ec-007`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`After 'provided (that)', use a present form for the future, just like after 'if'. Example: Provided that it is ready, we'll send it.`,wrongReasons:{"provided that the contract would be signed today, work can begin on monday":`'would' is not used after 'provided that'. Use the present passive: 'is signed'.`,"provided that the contract is sign today, work can begin on monday":`The passive needs the past participle: 'is signed', not 'is sign'.`},wrong:`Provided that the contract will be signed today, work can begin on Monday.`,solutions:[`Provided that the contract is signed today, work can begin on Monday.`,`Provided that the contract is signed today work can begin on Monday.`,`Provided the contract is signed today, work can begin on Monday.`,`Provided the contract is signed today work can begin on Monday.`]},{id:`b2-ec-008`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`In an inverted third conditional, 'Had' + subject + past participle replaces 'If I had...'. Example: Had we left earlier, we would have arrived on time.`,wrongReasons:{"had i know about the delay, i would have taken a later flight":`'Had' needs the past participle 'known', not the base form 'know'.`,"if i knew about the delay, i would have taken a later flight":`The past simple 'knew' refers to the present. For a past situation use the past perfect: 'If I had known'.`},wrong:`Had I knew about the delay, I would have taken a later flight.`,solutions:[`Had I known about the delay, I would have taken a later flight.`,`Had I known about the delay I would have taken a later flight.`,`Had I known about the delay, I'd have taken a later flight.`,`Had I known about the delay I'd have taken a later flight.`,`If I had known about the delay, I would have taken a later flight.`,`If I had known about the delay I would have taken a later flight.`,`If I had known about the delay, I'd have taken a later flight.`,`If I had known about the delay I'd have taken a later flight.`]},{id:`b2-ec-009`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'would' is a modal verb, so it is followed by the base form without 'to'. Example: If I were you, I would call him.`,wrongReasons:{"if i were you, i would apologising to her before the meeting":`After 'would' use the base form 'apologise', not the -ing form.`,"if i were you, i would to apologised to her before the meeting":`Remove 'to' and use the base form: 'would apologise'.`},wrong:`If I were you, I would to apologise to her before the meeting.`,solutions:[`If I were you, I would apologise to her before the meeting.`,`If I were you I would apologise to her before the meeting.`,`If I were you, I'd apologise to her before the meeting.`,`If I were you I'd apologise to her before the meeting.`,`If I were you, I would apologize to her before the meeting.`,`If I were you I would apologize to her before the meeting.`,`If I were you, I'd apologize to her before the meeting.`,`If I were you I'd apologize to her before the meeting.`]},{id:`b2-ec-010`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`In the if-clause of a third conditional, use the past perfect, never 'would have'. Example: She would have come if she hadn't been ill.`,wrongReasons:{"we would have won the match if our goalkeeper wasn't injured":`'wasn't' refers to the present or general time. The match is in the past, so use 'hadn't been injured'.`,"we would have won the match if our goalkeeper didn't been injured":`'did' cannot be used with 'been'. Use the past perfect: 'hadn't been injured'.`},wrong:`We would have won the match if our goalkeeper wouldn't have been injured.`,solutions:[`We would have won the match if our goalkeeper hadn't been injured.`,`We would have won the match if our goalkeeper had not been injured.`]},{id:`b2-ec-011`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Second conditional (imaginary situations): if + past simple, would + base form. Example: If I had a boat, I would sail around the world.`,wrongReasons:{"what would you do if you would win the lottery":`'would' is used in the result clause, not after 'if'. Use the past simple: 'if you won'.`,"what would you do if you win the lottery":`With 'would' in the result clause, the if-clause needs the past simple: 'if you won'.`},wrong:`What would you do if you will win the lottery?`,solutions:[`What would you do if you won the lottery?`]},{id:`b2-ec-012`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'As long as' works like 'if', so it takes the present simple for the future. Example: You can borrow it as long as you return it tomorrow.`,wrongReasons:{"as long as you would keep the receipt, you can return the jacket":`'would' is not used after 'as long as'. Use the present simple: 'you keep'.`,"as long as you kept the receipt, you can return the jacket":`This is a real future condition, so the present simple is needed: 'as long as you keep'.`},wrong:`As long as you will keep the receipt, you can return the jacket.`,solutions:[`As long as you keep the receipt, you can return the jacket.`,`As long as you keep the receipt you can return the jacket.`]},{id:`b2-ec-013`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The accident is in the past, so the result clause needs 'would have' + past participle. Example: If he had driven slowly, he would never have crashed.`,wrongReasons:{"if the bridge had been repaired last year, the accident would never happened":`After 'would' you need 'have' + past participle: 'would never have happened'.`,"if the bridge had been repaired last year, the accident will never have happened":`Use 'would', not 'will', in the result clause of a third conditional.`},wrong:`If the bridge had been repaired last year, the accident would never happen.`,solutions:[`If the bridge had been repaired last year, the accident would never have happened.`,`If the bridge had been repaired last year the accident would never have happened.`,`If the bridge had been repaired last year, the accident would have never happened.`,`If the bridge had been repaired last year the accident would have never happened.`]},{id:`b2-ec-014`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`For an imaginary past situation, the if-clause needs the past perfect. Example: If it hadn't snowed, the school wouldn't have closed.`,wrongReasons:{"if it didn't rain so heavily last night, the river wouldn't flood":`Both events are in the past ('last night'), so use 'hadn't rained' and 'wouldn't have flooded'.`,"if it hasn't rained so heavily last night, the river wouldn't have flooded":`The present perfect cannot be used with 'last night'. Use the past perfect: 'hadn't rained'.`},wrong:`If it didn't rain so heavily last night, the river wouldn't have flooded.`,solutions:[`If it hadn't rained so heavily last night, the river wouldn't have flooded.`,`If it had not rained so heavily last night the river would not have flooded.`,`If it had not rained so heavily last night the river wouldn't have flooded.`,`If it had not rained so heavily last night, the river would not have flooded.`,`If it had not rained so heavily last night, the river wouldn't have flooded.`,`If it hadn't rained so heavily last night the river would not have flooded.`,`If it hadn't rained so heavily last night the river wouldn't have flooded.`,`If it hadn't rained so heavily last night, the river would not have flooded.`]},{id:`b2-ec-015`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Supposing' works like 'if'. With a past verb (imaginary situation) the result uses 'would'. Example: Supposing you won a prize, what would you buy?`,wrongReasons:{"supposing you lost your passport abroad, what you would do":`In a direct question the auxiliary comes before the subject: 'what would you do?'.`,"supposing you lost your passport abroad, what would you did":`After 'would' use the base form 'do', not 'did'.`},wrong:`Supposing you lost your passport abroad, what will you do?`,solutions:[`Supposing you lost your passport abroad, what would you do?`,`Supposing you lost your passport abroad what would you do?`,`Supposing you lose your passport abroad, what will you do?`,`Supposing you lose your passport abroad what will you do?`]},{id:`b2-ec-016`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`For an unreal present situation, use the past simple after 'if' ('were' or 'was'). Example: If I were older, I could drive.`,wrongReasons:{"if i would be taller, i could join the basketball team":`'would' cannot be used in the if-clause. Use 'were' or 'was'.`,"if i be taller, i could join the basketball team":`'be' is not a correct verb form here. Use the past form: 'if I were taller'.`},wrong:`If I am taller, I could join the basketball team.`,solutions:[`If I were taller, I could join the basketball team.`,`If I were taller I could join the basketball team.`,`If I was taller, I could join the basketball team.`,`If I was taller I could join the basketball team.`]},{id:`b2-ec-017`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'tell' needs a person as object (told me/us); 'say' does not (said that). Example: He said that he was busy. / He told me that he was busy.`,wrongReasons:{"she told to me that she was tired of waiting":`'tell' takes the person directly, without 'to': 'she told me'.`,"she said me that she was tired of waiting":`'say' cannot be followed directly by a person. Use 'she said that' or 'she told me that'.`},wrong:`She told that she was tired of waiting.`,solutions:[`She said that she was tired of waiting.`,`She said she was tired of waiting.`,`She told me that she was tired of waiting.`,`She told us that she was tired of waiting.`,`She told him that she was tired of waiting.`,`She told her that she was tired of waiting.`,`She told them that she was tired of waiting.`,`She told me she was tired of waiting.`,`She told us she was tired of waiting.`,`She told him she was tired of waiting.`,`She told her she was tired of waiting.`,`She told them she was tired of waiting.`]},{id:`b2-ec-018`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Reported questions use normal statement word order, without 'do/did'. Example: She asked me what I wanted.`,wrongReasons:{"he asked me where do i live":`Reported questions do not use question word order or 'do'. Write 'where I lived'.`,"he asked me where i did live":`Do not add 'did' in a reported question. Use the past simple: 'where I lived'.`},wrong:`He asked me where did I live.`,solutions:[`He asked me where I lived.`,`He asked me where I live.`]},{id:`b2-ec-019`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`In reported speech about the past, 'can' usually changes to 'could'. Example: She asked if I could help her.`,wrongReasons:{"last month the manager asked if could we finish the report by the following friday":`Reported questions keep statement word order: 'if we could', not 'if could we'.`,"last month the manager asked if we can to finish the report by the following friday":`The tense is still wrong and modal verbs never take 'to'. Use 'if we could finish'.`},wrong:`Last month the manager asked if we can finish the report by the following Friday.`,solutions:[`Last month the manager asked if we could finish the report by the following Friday.`,`Last month the manager asked whether we could finish the report by the following Friday.`]},{id:`b2-ec-020`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Reported advice and orders use object + (not) to-infinitive. Example: She told me not to worry.`,wrongReasons:{"the doctor advised me to don't drink coffee late at night":`'don't' cannot follow 'to'. The negative infinitive is 'not to drink'.`,"the doctor advised me not drink coffee late at night":`You need 'to' after 'not': 'advised me not to drink'.`},wrong:`The doctor advised me don't drink coffee late at night.`,solutions:[`The doctor advised me not to drink coffee late at night.`]},{id:`b2-ec-021`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Reported yes/no questions use 'if' or 'whether', not 'that'. Example: He asked if I was ready.`,wrongReasons:{"she asked me had i seen her keys":`Reported questions do not use question word order. Use 'if I had seen'.`,"she asked me what i had seen her keys":`'what' is for wh-questions. A yes/no question is reported with 'if' or 'whether'.`},wrong:`She asked me that I had seen her keys.`,solutions:[`She asked me if I had seen her keys.`,`She asked me whether I had seen her keys.`]},{id:`b2-ec-022`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'tell + person' or 'say (to + person)'. 'say' is never followed directly by a person. Example: She told me she was hungry.`,wrongReasons:{"tom told to me he would call back later":`'tell' takes the person directly, without 'to': 'told me'.`,"tom said me that he would call back later":`The problem is still 'said me'. Use 'told me' or 'said to me'.`},wrong:`Tom said me he would call back later.`,solutions:[`Tom told me he would call back later.`,`Tom told me that he would call back later.`,`Tom told me he'd call back later.`,`Tom told me that he'd call back later.`,`Tom said he would call back later.`,`Tom said that he would call back later.`,`Tom said he'd call back later.`,`Tom said that he'd call back later.`,`Tom said to me he would call back later.`,`Tom said to me that he would call back later.`]},{id:`b2-ec-023`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'warn' in reported speech: warn + object + (not) to-infinitive. Example: They warned us not to swim there.`,wrongReasons:{"the guide warned us to not touching the paintings":`After 'to' use the base form 'touch', and the normal order is 'not to touch'.`,"the guide warned us don't touch the paintings":`Imperatives change to 'not to' + verb in reported speech: 'warned us not to touch'.`},wrong:`The guide warned us not touching the paintings.`,solutions:[`The guide warned us not to touch the paintings.`]},{id:`b2-ec-024`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`In reported speech after a past reporting verb, 'will' becomes 'would'. Example: He said he would phone me.`,wrongReasons:{"yesterday anna said she would sent the invitations the next day":`After 'would' use the base form 'send', not 'sent'.`,"yesterday anna said she sends the invitations the next day":`The present simple does not fit a future plan reported in the past. Use 'would send'.`},wrong:`Yesterday Anna said she will send the invitations the next day.`,solutions:[`Yesterday Anna said she would send the invitations the next day.`,`Yesterday Anna said that she would send the invitations the next day.`,`Yesterday Anna said she'd send the invitations the next day.`,`Yesterday Anna said that she'd send the invitations the next day.`]},{id:`b2-ec-025`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Reported questions use statement word order and no 'do/does'. Example: She asked what time the train left.`,wrongReasons:{"he wanted to know what time did the museum open":`Do not use 'did' in a reported question. Write 'what time the museum opened'.`,"he wanted to know what time the museum does open":`'does' is not needed in a reported question. Use 'opened' (or 'opens').`},wrong:`He wanted to know what time does the museum open.`,solutions:[`He wanted to know what time the museum opened.`,`He wanted to know what time the museum opens.`]},{id:`b2-ec-026`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'order' + object + to-infinitive (base form after 'to'). Example: The teacher ordered us to sit down.`,wrongReasons:{"the police officer ordered the driver get out of the car":`'order someone' needs 'to' before the verb: 'ordered the driver to get out'.`,"the police officer ordered to the driver to get out of the car":`'order' takes the person directly, without 'to': 'ordered the driver'.`},wrong:`The police officer ordered the driver to getting out of the car.`,solutions:[`The police officer ordered the driver to get out of the car.`]},{id:`b2-ec-027`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The reporting verb 'deny' is followed by an -ing form or a that-clause, not a to-infinitive. Example: She denied taking the money.`,wrongReasons:{"my brother denied to have broken the window":`'deny' never takes a to-infinitive. Use 'denied having broken' or 'denied breaking'.`,"my brother denied break the window":`After 'deny' use the -ing form: 'denied breaking'.`},wrong:`My brother denied to break the window.`,solutions:[`My brother denied breaking the window.`,`My brother denied having broken the window.`,`My brother denied that he had broken the window.`,`My brother denied that he broke the window.`]},{id:`b2-ec-028`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`After a preposition such as 'for', use the -ing form. Example: He apologised for forgetting my birthday.`,wrongReasons:{"she apologised to be late for the interview":`'apologise' needs 'for' + -ing: 'apologised for being late'.`,"she apologised for be late for the interview":`After the preposition 'for' use the -ing form 'being', not the base form.`},wrong:`She apologised for to be late for the interview.`,solutions:[`She apologised for being late for the interview.`,`She apologized for being late for the interview.`]},{id:`b2-ec-029`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'explain' cannot take the person directly; use 'explain (something) to someone'. Example: She explained to me how it worked.`,wrongReasons:{"the teacher explained us why the experiment failed":`The problem is still 'explained us'. Use 'explained to us'.`,"the teacher explained for us why the experiment had failed":`The correct preposition after 'explain' is 'to', not 'for'.`},wrong:`The teacher explained us why the experiment had failed.`,solutions:[`The teacher explained to us why the experiment had failed.`,`The teacher explained why the experiment had failed.`,`The teacher explained to us why the experiment failed.`,`The teacher explained why the experiment failed.`]},{id:`b2-ec-030`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`After a past reporting verb, the present perfect moves back to the past perfect. Example: She said she had finished the book.`,wrongReasons:{"last week mark told me he had lose his wallet the day before":`The past perfect needs the past participle: 'had lost'.`,"last week mark told me he has lose his wallet the day before":`'has lose' is wrong twice: use the past perfect with the participle, 'had lost'.`},wrong:`Last week Mark told me he has lost his wallet the day before.`,solutions:[`Last week Mark told me he had lost his wallet the day before.`,`Last week Mark told me that he had lost his wallet the day before.`,`Last week Mark told me he'd lost his wallet the day before.`,`Last week Mark told me that he'd lost his wallet the day before.`,`Last week Mark told me he lost his wallet the day before.`,`Last week Mark told me that he lost his wallet the day before.`]},{id:`b2-ec-031`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The passive is 'be' + past participle. The past participle of 'build' is 'built'. Example: The bridge was built in 1990.`,wrongReasons:{"the new hospital was builded in less than two years":`'build' is irregular: the past participle is 'built', not 'builded'.`,"the new hospital built in less than two years":`Without 'was' the sentence is active, as if the hospital built something. Use 'was built'.`},wrong:`The new hospital was build in less than two years.`,solutions:[`The new hospital was built in less than two years.`]},{id:`b2-ec-032`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The future passive is 'will be' + past participle. Example: The winner will be chosen tomorrow.`,wrongReasons:{"the results will be announce next monday":`After 'will be' you need the past participle 'announced'.`,"the results will being announced next monday":`After 'will' use the base form 'be', not 'being'.`},wrong:`The results will announced next Monday.`,solutions:[`The results will be announced next Monday.`]},{id:`b2-ec-033`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The present continuous passive is 'is/are being' + past participle. Example: My car is being fixed this week.`,wrongReasons:{"the bridge is repairing at the moment, so use the other road":`This is active: it says the bridge is repairing something. Use the passive 'is being repaired'.`,"the bridge is been repaired at the moment, so use the other road":`The continuous passive uses 'being', not 'been': 'is being repaired'.`},wrong:`The bridge is being repair at the moment, so use the other road.`,solutions:[`The bridge is being repaired at the moment, so use the other road.`,`The bridge is being repaired at the moment so use the other road.`]},{id:`b2-ec-034`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The present perfect passive is 'has/have been' + past participle. Example: The letter has just been sent.`,wrongReasons:{"the suspect has just arrested by the police":`Without 'been' this is active. The passive needs 'has just been arrested'.`,"the suspect has just been arresting by the police":`The passive uses the past participle 'arrested', not the -ing form.`},wrong:`The suspect has just been arrest by the police.`,solutions:[`The suspect has just been arrested by the police.`]},{id:`b2-ec-035`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The passive needs the past participle. 'speak - spoke - spoken'. Example: French is spoken in Canada.`,wrongReasons:{"english is speaking in many countries around the world":`'is speaking' is active (English is doing the speaking). Use the passive 'is spoken'.`,"english is spoked in many countries around the world":`'speak' is irregular: the past participle is 'spoken', not 'spoked'.`},wrong:`English is spoke in many countries around the world.`,solutions:[`English is spoken in many countries around the world.`]},{id:`b2-ec-036`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Passive = 'be' + past participle. 'steal - stole - stolen'. Example: Her bag was stolen on the train.`,wrongReasons:{"my car was stealed while i was at the cinema":`'steal' is irregular: the past participle is 'stolen'.`,"my car stole while i was at the cinema":`This is active, as if the car stole something. Use the passive 'was stolen'.`},wrong:`My car was stole while I was at the cinema.`,solutions:[`My car was stolen while I was at the cinema.`]},{id:`b2-ec-037`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The causative is 'have/get + object + past participle' when someone does a service for you. Example: We had our windows cleaned.`,wrongReasons:{"i had cut my hair yesterday":`This past perfect means you cut your hair yourself. For a service use 'had my hair cut'.`,"i had my hair cutted yesterday":`'cut' is irregular: the past participle is 'cut', not 'cutted'.`},wrong:`I had my hair cutting yesterday.`,solutions:[`I had my hair cut yesterday.`,`I got my hair cut yesterday.`]},{id:`b2-ec-038`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`For a past action after 'is believed/said/thought', use 'to have been' + past participle. Example: The castle is thought to have been built in 1200.`,wrongReasons:{"the painting is believed to painted in the 1600s":`After 'to' you need a verb form: 'to have been painted' for a past passive action.`,"the painting is believed to have painted in the 1600s":`'to have painted' is active (the painting painted something). Add 'been': 'to have been painted'.`},wrong:`The painting is believed to be painted in the 1600s.`,solutions:[`The painting is believed to have been painted in the 1600s.`]},{id:`b2-ec-039`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The impersonal passive is 'It is said/believed/thought that...'. Example: It is said that the hotel is haunted.`,wrongReasons:{"it is saying that the company is losing money":`The impersonal structure is passive: 'It is said', not 'It is saying'.`,"it said that the company is losing money":`'It said' needs a real speaker (like a report). For general opinion use 'It is said that'.`},wrong:`It is say that the company is losing money.`,solutions:[`It is said that the company is losing money.`]},{id:`b2-ec-040`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`A modal passive is modal + 'be' + past participle. Example: The form must be completed in pen.`,wrongReasons:{"the documents must be signing by both parties":`The passive needs the past participle 'signed', not the -ing form.`,"the documents must signed by both parties":`You need 'be' after the modal: 'must be signed'.`},wrong:`The documents must be sign by both parties.`,solutions:[`The documents must be signed by both parties.`]},{id:`b2-ec-041`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The past continuous passive is 'was/were being' + past participle. Example: The room was being cleaned when I arrived.`,wrongReasons:{"the letters were delivering when the storm started":`This is active, as if the letters were delivering something. Use 'were being delivered'.`,"the letters were been delivered when the storm started":`The continuous passive uses 'being': 'were being delivered'.`},wrong:`The letters were being deliver when the storm started.`,solutions:[`The letters were being delivered when the storm started.`]},{id:`b2-ec-042`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The past perfect passive is 'had been' + past participle. Example: The tickets had been sold before we arrived.`,wrongReasons:{"the road had being closed before the snow started":`After 'had' use the past participle 'been', not 'being'.`,"the road had been close before the snow started":`The passive needs the past participle 'closed', not the base form.`},wrong:`The road had been closing before the snow started.`,solutions:[`The road had been closed before the snow started.`]},{id:`b2-ec-043`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The 'going to' passive is 'going to be' + past participle. Example: The old school is going to be knocked down.`,wrongReasons:{"our kitchen is going to be paint next week":`After 'be' the passive needs the past participle 'painted'.`,"our kitchen is going to painting next week":`After 'going to' you need 'be' + past participle: 'going to be painted'.`},wrong:`Our kitchen is going to painted next week.`,solutions:[`Our kitchen is going to be painted next week.`]},{id:`b2-ec-044`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Active 'make someone do' becomes passive 'be made to do' (with 'to'). Example: We were made to wait for an hour.`,wrongReasons:{"the children were made tidying their rooms before dinner":`In the passive, 'made' is followed by a to-infinitive: 'were made to tidy'.`,"the children were make to tidy their rooms before dinner":`The passive needs the past participle 'made', not 'make'.`},wrong:`The children were made tidy their rooms before dinner.`,solutions:[`The children were made to tidy their rooms before dinner.`]},{id:`b2-ec-045`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'who' or 'that' for people and 'which' for things. Example: The man who called you is my uncle.`,wrongReasons:{"the woman whom lives next door is a famous pianist":`'whom' is an object pronoun. Here the woman is the subject of 'lives', so use 'who'.`,"the woman whose lives next door is a famous pianist":`'whose' shows possession (whose car). Here you need the subject pronoun 'who'.`},wrong:`The woman which lives next door is a famous pianist.`,solutions:[`The woman who lives next door is a famous pianist.`,`The woman that lives next door is a famous pianist.`]},{id:`b2-ec-046`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'whose' + noun for possession in a relative clause. Example: I know a girl whose father is a pilot.`,wrongReasons:{"that's the man who's car was stolen last week":`'who's' means 'who is'. The possessive form is 'whose'.`,"that's the man which car was stolen last week":`'which' is for things and does not show possession. Use 'whose car'.`},wrong:`That's the man who his car was stolen last week.`,solutions:[`That's the man whose car was stolen last week.`,`That is the man whose car was stolen last week.`]},{id:`b2-ec-047`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'that' cannot be used in an extra-information clause (between commas); use 'which' for things. Example: My car, which is very old, still runs well.`,wrongReasons:{"paris, who is the capital of france, attracts millions of tourists":`'who' is for people. For a city use 'which'.`,"paris which is the capital of france attracts millions of tourists":`This extra information about a unique place needs commas: 'Paris, which is..., attracts'.`},wrong:`Paris, that is the capital of France, attracts millions of tourists.`,solutions:[`Paris, which is the capital of France, attracts millions of tourists.`]},{id:`b2-ec-048`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'where' already means 'in which', so do not add another preposition. Example: That's the café where we met.`,wrongReasons:{"this is the hotel where we stayed at last summer":`'where' already contains the preposition. Do not add 'at' or 'in' after the verb.`,"this is the hotel which we stayed last summer":`With 'which' you need the preposition: 'which we stayed in' (or use 'where').`},wrong:`This is the hotel where we stayed in last summer.`,solutions:[`This is the hotel where we stayed last summer.`,`This is the hotel which we stayed in last summer.`,`This is the hotel that we stayed in last summer.`,`This is the hotel we stayed in last summer.`,`This is the hotel in which we stayed last summer.`]},{id:`b2-ec-049`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'what' is not a relative pronoun after a noun. Use 'that' or 'which', or leave it out when it is the object. Example: The film (that) we saw was great.`,wrongReasons:{"the book whom i borrowed from the library is overdue":`'whom' is only for people. For a book use 'that' or 'which'.`,"the book who i borrowed from the library is overdue":`'who' refers to people, not things. Use 'that' or 'which'.`},wrong:`The book what I borrowed from the library is overdue.`,solutions:[`The book that I borrowed from the library is overdue.`,`The book which I borrowed from the library is overdue.`,`The book I borrowed from the library is overdue.`]},{id:`b2-ec-050`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The relative pronoun replaces the subject, so do not repeat it with 'he/she/it'. Example: My aunt, who lives in Rome, is a nurse.`,wrongReasons:{"my grandfather, that fought in the war, rarely talks about it":`'that' cannot introduce an extra-information clause between commas. Use 'who'.`,"my grandfather who he fought in the war rarely talks about it":`'he' is still there. 'who' is already the subject, so remove 'he' (and keep the commas).`},wrong:`My grandfather, who he fought in the war, rarely talks about it.`,solutions:[`My grandfather, who fought in the war, rarely talks about it.`]},{id:`b2-ec-051`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`After 'the reason', use 'why' or 'that' (or nothing), not 'because'. Example: The reason why I called is to say thank you.`,wrongReasons:{"the reason which she left is still a mystery":`'which' cannot follow 'the reason' like this. Use 'why' or 'that'.`,"the reason of why she left is still a mystery":`Do not add 'of'. Use 'the reason why she left'.`},wrong:`The reason because she left is still a mystery.`,solutions:[`The reason why she left is still a mystery.`,`The reason that she left is still a mystery.`,`The reason she left is still a mystery.`]},{id:`b2-ec-052`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Directly after a preposition, use 'whom' for people (not 'who' or 'that'). Example: The person to whom I spoke was very kind.`,wrongReasons:{"the scientist to that the prize was awarded gave a short speech":`'that' can never follow a preposition. Use 'to whom'.`,"the scientist to which the prize was awarded gave a short speech":`'which' is for things. For a person after a preposition use 'whom'.`},wrong:`The scientist to who the prize was awarded gave a short speech.`,solutions:[`The scientist to whom the prize was awarded gave a short speech.`,`The scientist who the prize was awarded to gave a short speech.`,`The scientist whom the prize was awarded to gave a short speech.`,`The scientist that the prize was awarded to gave a short speech.`,`The scientist the prize was awarded to gave a short speech.`]},{id:`b2-ec-053`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`To comment on a whole clause, use ', which'. 'that' is not possible after a comma. Example: She passed the exam, which pleased her parents.`,wrongReasons:{"he failed his driving test three times, what surprised everyone":`'what' cannot refer back to a clause. Use ', which surprised everyone'.`,"he failed his driving test three times, who surprised everyone":`'who' is for people. To refer to the whole situation, use 'which'.`},wrong:`He failed his driving test three times, that surprised everyone.`,solutions:[`He failed his driving test three times, which surprised everyone.`]},{id:`b2-ec-054`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`After a relative clause, the main verb follows directly; do not repeat the subject with 'it'. Example: The town where I live is quiet.`,wrongReasons:{"the village which i grew up has changed a lot":`With 'which' you need the preposition: 'which I grew up in' (or use 'where').`,"the village where i grew up in has changed a lot":`'where' already means 'in which', so do not add 'in'.`},wrong:`The village where I grew up it has changed a lot.`,solutions:[`The village where I grew up has changed a lot.`,`The village I grew up in has changed a lot.`,`The village that I grew up in has changed a lot.`,`The village which I grew up in has changed a lot.`,`The village in which I grew up has changed a lot.`]},{id:`b2-ec-055`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`After a preposition use 'which' (things) or 'whom' (people), never 'that'. Or put the preposition at the end. Example: The team for which she plays won.`,wrongReasons:{"the company for who i work has offices in five countries":`'who' is for people. A company after a preposition takes 'which'.`,"the company for what i work has offices in five countries":`'what' is not a relative pronoun after a noun. Use 'for which'.`},wrong:`The company for that I work has offices in five countries.`,solutions:[`The company for which I work has offices in five countries.`,`The company that I work for has offices in five countries.`,`The company which I work for has offices in five countries.`,`The company I work for has offices in five countries.`]},{id:`b2-ec-056`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The relative pronoun is already the object, so do not add 'it' at the end. Example: This is the key that I lost.`,wrongReasons:{"is this the phone what you were looking for":`'what' cannot follow a noun as a relative pronoun. Use 'that' or 'which'.`,"is this the phone that you were looking it":`'look for' needs its preposition, and 'it' repeats the object. Write 'that you were looking for'.`},wrong:`Is this the phone that you were looking for it?`,solutions:[`Is this the phone that you were looking for?`,`Is this the phone which you were looking for?`,`Is this the phone you were looking for?`]},{id:`b2-ec-057`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The verb in a relative clause agrees with the noun the pronoun refers to. Example: He's the one who knows the answer.`,wrongReasons:{"she's the only person whom understands me":`'whom' is an object pronoun; here you need the subject 'who'. The verb also needs -s.`,"she's the only person who understanding me":`A relative clause needs a full verb: 'who understands me'.`},wrong:`She's the only person who understand me.`,solutions:[`She's the only person who understands me.`,`She is the only person who understands me.`,`She's the only person that understands me.`,`She is the only person that understands me.`]},{id:`b2-ec-058`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'visit' takes a direct object, so use 'which' (not 'where'). 'where' means 'in/at which'. Example: The city, which we visited in May, was lovely.`,wrongReasons:{"the museum, that we visited yesterday, has a fantastic dinosaur collection":`'that' is not used in extra-information clauses (between commas). Use 'which'.`,"the museum, who we visited yesterday, has a fantastic dinosaur collection":`'who' is for people. For a museum use 'which'.`},wrong:`The museum, where we visited yesterday, has a fantastic dinosaur collection.`,solutions:[`The museum, which we visited yesterday, has a fantastic dinosaur collection.`,`The museum, where we went yesterday, has a fantastic dinosaur collection.`]},{id:`b2-ec-059`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`For a present wish about ability, use 'wish + could'. Example: I wish I could play the guitar.`,wrongReasons:{"i wish i would speak japanese":`We do not use 'would' to wish about our own abilities. Use 'could'.`,"i wish i will speak japanese":`'will' is not used after 'wish'. Use 'could speak'.`},wrong:`I wish I can speak Japanese.`,solutions:[`I wish I could speak Japanese.`]},{id:`b2-ec-060`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`To express regret about the past, use 'wish + past perfect'. Example: I wish I had stayed at home.`,wrongReasons:{"i wish i haven't eaten so much at the party last night":`The present perfect cannot go with 'last night'. Use 'hadn't eaten'.`,"i wish i wouldn't have eaten so much at the party last night":`'would have' is not used after 'wish' for past regrets. Use 'hadn't eaten'.`},wrong:`I wish I didn't eat so much at the party last night.`,solutions:[`I wish I hadn't eaten so much at the party last night.`,`I wish I had not eaten so much at the party last night.`]},{id:`b2-ec-061`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'If only' + past simple expresses a strong wish about the present. Example: If only I had more money!`,wrongReasons:{"if only i would know her phone number":`'would' is not used with a state verb like 'know' here. Use 'knew'.`,"if only i had know her phone number":`'had' needs the past participle 'known'; but for a present wish, simply use 'knew'.`},wrong:`If only I know her phone number!`,solutions:[`If only I knew her phone number!`]},{id:`b2-ec-062`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'wish + would' to complain about other people's annoying behaviour. Example: I wish he would stop interrupting me.`,wrongReasons:{"i wish my neighbours stop playing loud music at night":`After 'wish' we do not use the present simple. Use 'would stop'.`,"i wish my neighbours would stopped playing loud music at night":`After 'would' use the base form 'stop'.`},wrong:`I wish my neighbours will stop playing loud music at night.`,solutions:[`I wish my neighbours would stop playing loud music at night.`,`I wish my neighbors would stop playing loud music at night.`]},{id:`b2-ec-063`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`For a present wish, use 'wish + past simple'. Example: He wishes he lived by the sea.`,wrongReasons:{"she wishes she would have more free time":`Do not use 'would' to wish about your own situation. Use 'had'.`,"she wish she had more free time":`With 'she', the verb 'wish' needs -es: 'she wishes'.`},wrong:`She wishes she has more free time.`,solutions:[`She wishes she had more free time.`]},{id:`b2-ec-064`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`For past regrets, use 'wish + past perfect', not 'would have'. Example: She wishes she had taken the job.`,wrongReasons:{"i wish i studied harder for my exams last year":`The past simple after 'wish' refers to the present. For 'last year' use 'had studied'.`,"i wish i have studied harder for my exams last year":`The present perfect is not used after 'wish'. Use 'had studied'.`},wrong:`I wish I would have studied harder for my exams last year.`,solutions:[`I wish I had studied harder for my exams last year.`,`I wish I'd studied harder for my exams last year.`]},{id:`b2-ec-065`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'If only' + past perfect expresses regret about the past. Example: If only I had listened to you!`,wrongReasons:{"if only we haven't missed the last bus yesterday":`The present perfect does not go with 'yesterday'. Use 'hadn't missed'.`,"if only we wouldn't have missed the last bus yesterday":`'would have' is not used after 'if only'. Use 'hadn't missed'.`},wrong:`If only we didn't miss the last bus yesterday!`,solutions:[`If only we hadn't missed the last bus yesterday!`,`If only we had not missed the last bus yesterday!`]},{id:`b2-ec-066`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`After 'wish', move the present back one tense: 'are coming' becomes 'were coming'. Example: I wish she were staying longer.`,wrongReasons:{"i wish you will come with us to the concert tonight":`'will' is not used after 'wish'. Use 'were coming' or 'could come'.`,"i wish you was coming with us to the concert tonight":`With 'you' the past form of 'be' is always 'were'.`},wrong:`I wish you are coming with us to the concert tonight.`,solutions:[`I wish you were coming with us to the concert tonight.`,`I wish you could come with us to the concert tonight.`]},{id:`b2-ec-067`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`A present wish uses the past form of 'be' ('were' or 'was'). Example: I wish I weren't so tired.`,wrongReasons:{"i wish i wouldn't be so nervous before job interviews":`Do not use 'would' to wish about your own feelings. Use 'weren't' or 'wasn't'.`,"i wish i don't be so nervous before job interviews":`'don't be' is not possible here. Use the past form: 'weren't'.`},wrong:`I wish I am not so nervous before job interviews.`,solutions:[`I wish I weren't so nervous before job interviews.`,`I wish I were not so nervous before job interviews.`,`I wish I wasn't so nervous before job interviews.`,`I wish I was not so nervous before job interviews.`]},{id:`b2-ec-068`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`For a wish about your own ability, use 'could' or 'were/was able to', not 'would'. Example: I wish I could fly.`,wrongReasons:{"he wishes he can afford a bigger flat":`After 'wish', 'can' moves back to 'could'.`,"he wishes he will be able to afford a bigger flat":`'will' is not used after 'wish'. Use 'could afford'.`},wrong:`He wishes he would be able to afford a bigger flat.`,solutions:[`He wishes he could afford a bigger flat.`,`He wishes he were able to afford a bigger flat.`,`He wishes he was able to afford a bigger flat.`]},{id:`b2-ec-069`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'would rather' + another person takes the past simple, even for the present or future. Example: I'd rather you came tomorrow.`,wrongReasons:{"i'd rather you won't tell anyone about this":`'will' is not used after 'would rather + person'. Use 'didn't tell'.`,"i'd rather you not to tell anyone about this":`Do not use 'to' here. Use 'you didn't tell'.`},wrong:`I'd rather you don't tell anyone about this.`,solutions:[`I'd rather you didn't tell anyone about this.`,`I'd rather you did not tell anyone about this.`,`I would rather you didn't tell anyone about this.`,`I would rather you did not tell anyone about this.`]},{id:`b2-ec-070`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'If only' + past perfect ('had' + past participle) for past regrets; do not add 'have'. Example: If only we had left earlier.`,wrongReasons:{"if only i have listened to your advice":`The present perfect is not used after 'if only'. Use 'had listened'.`,"if only i had listen to your advice":`After 'had' you need the past participle 'listened'.`},wrong:`If only I had have listened to your advice.`,solutions:[`If only I had listened to your advice.`,`If only I'd listened to your advice.`]},{id:`b2-ec-071`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'It's (high) time' + subject + past simple means something should happen now. Example: It's time you went to bed.`,wrongReasons:{"it's high time we will leave for the airport":`'will' is not used after 'it's high time'. Use the past simple 'left'.`,"it's high time we had left for the airport":`Use the past simple, not the past perfect: 'it's high time we left'.`},wrong:`It's high time we leave for the airport.`,solutions:[`It's high time we left for the airport.`,`It is high time we left for the airport.`,`It's high time for us to leave for the airport.`,`It is high time for us to leave for the airport.`]},{id:`b2-ec-072`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'must' when you are sure something is true and 'can't' when you are sure it is not true. Example: He can't be hungry; he has just eaten.`,wrongReasons:{"she mustn't be at home now; her car isn't in the driveway":`'mustn't' means 'it is not allowed'. For a negative deduction use 'can't'.`,"she doesn't have to be at home now; her car isn't in the driveway":`'doesn't have to' means 'it isn't necessary'. For a deduction use 'can't be'.`},wrong:`She must be at home now; her car isn't in the driveway.`,solutions:[`She can't be at home now; her car isn't in the driveway.`,`She can't be at home now; her car is not in the driveway.`,`She cannot be at home now; her car is not in the driveway.`,`She cannot be at home now; her car isn't in the driveway.`,`She couldn't be at home now; her car isn't in the driveway.`,`She could not be at home now; her car is not in the driveway.`,`She could not be at home now; her car isn't in the driveway.`,`She couldn't be at home now; her car is not in the driveway.`]},{id:`b2-ec-073`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'mustn't' means something is forbidden; 'don't have to' / 'needn't' mean it is not necessary. Example: You don't have to come if you're busy.`,wrongReasons:{"you haven't to pay for the tickets because they're free":`'have to' makes its negative with 'do': 'don't have to'.`,"you don't must pay for the tickets because they're free":`'must' never uses 'do'. Use 'don't have to' or 'needn't'.`},wrong:`You mustn't pay for the tickets because they're free.`,solutions:[`You don't have to pay for the tickets because they're free.`,`You do not have to pay for the tickets because they are free.`,`You do not have to pay for the tickets because they're free.`,`You don't have to pay for the tickets because they are free.`,`You needn't pay for the tickets because they're free.`,`You need not pay for the tickets because they are free.`,`You need not pay for the tickets because they're free.`,`You needn't pay for the tickets because they are free.`,`You don't need to pay for the tickets because they're free.`,`You do not need to pay for the tickets because they are free.`,`You do not need to pay for the tickets because they're free.`,`You don't need to pay for the tickets because they are free.`]},{id:`b2-ec-074`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Past deduction uses modal + 'have' + past participle. 'forget - forgot - forgotten'. Example: She can't have seen us.`,wrongReasons:{"he can't have forget about the meeting; i reminded him this morning":`After 'have' you need the past participle 'forgotten'.`,"he can't forgot about the meeting; i reminded him this morning":`A past deduction needs 'have' + past participle: 'can't have forgotten'.`},wrong:`He can't have forgot about the meeting; I reminded him this morning.`,solutions:[`He can't have forgotten about the meeting; I reminded him this morning.`,`He cannot have forgotten about the meeting; I reminded him this morning.`,`He couldn't have forgotten about the meeting; I reminded him this morning.`,`He could not have forgotten about the meeting; I reminded him this morning.`]},{id:`b2-ec-075`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`To criticise a past action, use 'should have' + past participle. Example: You should have told me earlier.`,wrongReasons:{"you should have call me when you arrived":`After 'have' you need the past participle 'called'.`,"you should to have called me when you arrived":`'should' is a modal verb, so it is never followed by 'to'.`},wrong:`You should called me when you arrived.`,solutions:[`You should have called me when you arrived.`]},{id:`b2-ec-076`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Past possibility uses 'might/may/could have' + past participle. 'take - took - taken'. Example: He might have missed the bus.`,wrongReasons:{"they might took the wrong train":`A modal is followed by the base form; for the past, use 'have' + past participle: 'might have taken'.`,"they might have take the wrong train":`After 'have' use the past participle 'taken', not the base form.`},wrong:`They might have took the wrong train.`,solutions:[`They might have taken the wrong train.`]},{id:`b2-ec-077`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`As a modal, 'needn't' is followed by the base form without 'to'. 'don't need' takes 'to'. Example: You needn't wait for me.`,wrongReasons:{"we don't need hurry; the film doesn't start until nine":`'don't need' is an ordinary verb, so it needs 'to': 'don't need to hurry'.`,"we needn't hurrying; the film doesn't start until nine":`After 'needn't' use the base form 'hurry'.`},wrong:`We needn't to hurry; the film doesn't start until nine.`,solutions:[`We needn't hurry; the film doesn't start until nine.`,`We need not hurry; the film does not start until nine.`,`We need not hurry; the film doesn't start until nine.`,`We needn't hurry; the film does not start until nine.`,`We don't need to hurry; the film doesn't start until nine.`,`We do not need to hurry; the film does not start until nine.`,`We do not need to hurry; the film doesn't start until nine.`,`We don't need to hurry; the film does not start until nine.`]},{id:`b2-ec-078`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`For general ability in the past, use 'could' (or 'was able to'). Example: My grandmother could speak four languages.`,wrongReasons:{"when i was a child, i could to swim across the lake":`'could' is a modal verb, so it is not followed by 'to'.`,"when i was a child, i can swam across the lake":`Change the modal, not the main verb: 'could swim'.`},wrong:`When I was a child, I can swim across the lake.`,solutions:[`When I was a child, I could swim across the lake.`,`When I was a child I could swim across the lake.`,`When I was a child, I was able to swim across the lake.`,`When I was a child I was able to swim across the lake.`]},{id:`b2-ec-079`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'had better' is followed by the base form without 'to'. Example: We'd better leave now.`,wrongReasons:{"you had better wearing a helmet when you ride that bike":`After 'had better' use the base form 'wear', not the -ing form.`,"you would better wear a helmet when you ride that bike":`The expression is 'had better' ('d better), not 'would better'.`},wrong:`You had better to wear a helmet when you ride that bike.`,solutions:[`You had better wear a helmet when you ride that bike.`,`You'd better wear a helmet when you ride that bike.`]},{id:`b2-ec-080`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'must' and 'must not' are followed by the base form without 'to'. Example: You must not park here.`,wrongReasons:{"visitors don't must take photographs inside the gallery":`'must' does not use 'do' for its negative. Use 'must not' or 'mustn't'.`,"visitors must not taking photographs inside the gallery":`After a modal use the base form 'take'.`},wrong:`Visitors must not to take photographs inside the gallery.`,solutions:[`Visitors must not take photographs inside the gallery.`,`Visitors mustn't take photographs inside the gallery.`]},{id:`b2-ec-081`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`To be sure about a past event, use 'must have' + past participle. Example: She must have left early.`,wrongReasons:{"the road is wet, so it must had rained last night":`After a modal use 'have', not 'had': 'must have rained'.`,"the road is wet, so it must rained last night":`A past deduction needs 'have' + past participle: 'must have rained'.`},wrong:`The road is wet, so it must rain last night.`,solutions:[`The road is wet, so it must have rained last night.`,`The road is wet so it must have rained last night.`]},{id:`b2-ec-082`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Two modals cannot go together. For future ability use 'will be able to'. Example: Will you be able to help me tomorrow?`,wrongReasons:{"will you able to come to my party on saturday":`You need 'be' before 'able': 'will you be able to come'.`,"will you could come to my party on saturday":`'will' and 'could' are both modals and cannot be used together.`},wrong:`Will you can come to my party on Saturday?`,solutions:[`Will you be able to come to my party on Saturday?`,`Can you come to my party on Saturday?`]},{id:`b2-ec-083`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'ought to' is followed by the base form of the verb. Example: You ought to see a doctor.`,wrongReasons:{"you ought return the book to the library by friday":`'ought' always needs 'to': 'ought to return'.`,"you ought to returned the book to the library by friday":`After 'ought to' use the base form 'return', not the past form.`},wrong:`You ought to returning the book to the library by Friday.`,solutions:[`You ought to return the book to the library by Friday.`]},{id:`b2-ec-084`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'must' has no past form; for past obligation use 'had to' (question: 'Did you have to...?'). Example: Did you have to work last weekend?`,wrongReasons:{"did you had to wear a uniform at your old school":`After 'did' use the base form 'have', not 'had'.`,"must you wear a uniform at your old school":`'must' refers to the present. For the past, use 'Did you have to'.`},wrong:`Did you must wear a uniform at your old school?`,solutions:[`Did you have to wear a uniform at your old school?`]},{id:`b2-ec-085`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Look up' means to search for information in a book or online; 'look after' means to take care of someone. Example: I looked up the address online.`,wrongReasons:{"could you look after the word in the dictionary for me":`'Look after' means to take care of a person or thing, not to search for information. Use 'look up'.`},wrong:`Could you look after the word in the dictionary for me?`,solutions:[`Could you look up the word in the dictionary for me?`,`Could you look the word up in the dictionary for me?`]},{id:`b2-ec-086`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Put up with' (= tolerate) is a three-part phrasal verb and needs 'with' before the object. Example: I can't put up with his rudeness.`,wrongReasons:{"i can't put up the noise from the building site any longer":`'Put up' without 'with' means to build or to give someone a bed; to mean 'tolerate' you need 'put up with'.`,"i can't put up to the noise from the building site any longer":`The preposition is 'with', not 'to': 'put up with' something.`},wrong:`I can't put up the noise from the building site any longer.`,solutions:[`I can't put up with the noise from the building site any longer.`,`I cannot put up with the noise from the building site any longer.`]},{id:`b2-ec-087`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Turn down' means to refuse an offer or request; 'turn off' is for machines and lights. Example: She turned down the invitation.`,wrongReasons:{"he turned the job offer off because the salary was too low":`'Turn off' is used for lights, taps and machines; to refuse an offer we say 'turn down'.`,"he turned the job offer out because the salary was too low":`'Turn out' means to produce or to have a particular result; it does not mean 'refuse'.`},wrong:`He turned the job offer off because the salary was too low.`,solutions:[`He turned the job offer down because the salary was too low.`,`He turned down the job offer because the salary was too low.`]},{id:`b2-ec-088`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`When a machine or vehicle stops working, it 'breaks down'. Example: The lift broke down again this morning.`,wrongReasons:{"our car broke out on the motorway, so we called a mechanic":`'Break out' is used for wars, fires or diseases that start suddenly, not for cars that stop working.`,"our car broke off on the motorway, so we called a mechanic":`'Break off' means to separate a piece from something or to end a relationship; a car that stops working 'breaks down'.`},wrong:`Our car broke out on the motorway, so we called a mechanic.`,solutions:[`Our car broke down on the motorway, so we called a mechanic.`]},{id:`b2-ec-089`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Look into' means to investigate or examine a problem. Example: The council is looking into our complaint.`,wrongReasons:{"the insurance company is looking up my claim":`'Look up' means to find information in a reference source; to investigate a problem, use 'look into'.`,"the insurance company is looking after my claim":`'Look after' means to take care of someone; it does not mean 'investigate'.`},wrong:`The insurance company is looking up my claim.`,solutions:[`The insurance company is looking into my claim.`]},{id:`b2-ec-090`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Bring up' (transitive) means to raise a child; 'grow up' (intransitive) is what the child does. Example: I grew up in Wales because my grandparents brought me up there.`,wrongReasons:{"my aunt grew me up after my parents moved abroad":`'Grow up' has no object: a child grows up. The adult who raises the child 'brings them up'.`,"my aunt brought up me after my parents moved abroad":`With a pronoun object, the pronoun goes between the verb and the particle: 'brought me up'.`},wrong:`My aunt grew me up after my parents moved abroad.`,solutions:[`My aunt brought me up after my parents moved abroad.`,`My aunt raised me after my parents moved abroad.`]},{id:`b2-ec-091`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`With separable phrasal verbs, an object pronoun must go between the verb and the particle. Example: I'll pick you up at eight.`,wrongReasons:{"can you pick up me from the station at six":`A pronoun object cannot follow the particle: say 'pick me up', not 'pick up me'.`,"can you pick me from the station at six":`Without 'up', 'pick' means to choose; to collect someone by car, you need 'pick up'.`},wrong:`Can you pick up me from the station at six?`,solutions:[`Can you pick me up from the station at six?`]},{id:`b2-ec-092`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Run out of' something means to have none left. Example: The printer has run out of paper.`,wrongReasons:{"we have run out from milk, so i'll go to the shop":`The phrasal verb is 'run out of', not 'run out from'.`,"we have run off milk, so i'll go to the shop":`'Run off' means to leave quickly or escape; when you have none left, you have 'run out of' it.`},wrong:`We have run out from milk, so I'll go to the shop.`,solutions:[`We have run out of milk, so I'll go to the shop.`,`We've run out of milk, so I'll go to the shop.`,`We have run out of milk, so I will go to the shop.`,`We've run out of milk, so I will go to the shop.`]},{id:`b2-ec-093`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Find out' means to discover a fact or piece of information. Example: Did you find out who sent the flowers?`,wrongReasons:{"i need to find up what time the museum opens":`There is no phrasal verb 'find up'; to discover information, use 'find out'.`,"i need to find about what time the museum opens":`'Find' needs the particle 'out' here: 'find out' what time something happens.`},wrong:`I need to find up what time the museum opens.`,solutions:[`I need to find out what time the museum opens.`]},{id:`b2-ec-094`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`You 'fill in' or 'fill out' a form, meaning you write the information it asks for. Example: Fill in your name and address.`,wrongReasons:{"please fill on this form and return it to reception":`The particle is wrong: we 'fill in' or 'fill out' a form, never 'fill on'.`,"please fill up this form and return it to reception":`'Fill up' means to make something completely full, like a glass or a tank, not to complete a form.`},wrong:`Please fill on this form and return it to reception.`,solutions:[`Please fill in this form and return it to reception.`,`Please fill out this form and return it to reception.`,`Please fill this form in and return it to reception.`,`Please fill this form out and return it to reception.`]},{id:`b2-ec-095`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Get over' means to recover from an illness or a bad experience and takes a direct object, with no preposition. Example: He never got over the shock.`,wrongReasons:{"my brother still hasn't got over from his food poisoning":`'Get over' is followed directly by its object; do not add 'from'.`,"my brother still hasn't got over of his food poisoning":`No preposition follows 'get over': you get over an illness, not 'get over of' it.`},wrong:`My brother still hasn't got over from his food poisoning.`,solutions:[`My brother still hasn't got over his food poisoning.`,`My brother still has not got over his food poisoning.`,`My brother still hasn't gotten over his food poisoning.`,`My brother still has not gotten over his food poisoning.`]},{id:`b2-ec-096`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`When an alarm or bomb starts ringing or explodes, it 'goes off'. Example: My alarm clock went off at six.`,wrongReasons:{"the fire alarm went out during the exam":`'Go out' is used for lights and fires that stop burning; an alarm that starts ringing 'goes off'.`,"the fire alarm went on during the exam":`'Go on' means to continue; an alarm that suddenly starts ringing 'goes off'.`},wrong:`The fire alarm went out during the exam.`,solutions:[`The fire alarm went off during the exam.`]},{id:`b2-ec-097`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Come across' means to find something by chance. Example: I came across a great café near the river.`,wrongReasons:{"while cleaning the attic, i came over some old letters from my grandfather":`'Come over' means to visit someone's home; to find something by chance, use 'come across'.`,"while cleaning the attic, i came up some old letters from my grandfather":`'Come up' means to be mentioned or to happen; it cannot take an object meaning 'find'.`},wrong:`While cleaning the attic, I came over some old letters from my grandfather.`,solutions:[`While cleaning the attic, I came across some old letters from my grandfather.`]},{id:`b2-ec-098`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Set off' or 'set out' means to start a journey. Example: They set off early to avoid the traffic.`,wrongReasons:{"we set up for the coast at dawn":`'Set up' means to start a business or prepare equipment, not to begin a journey.`,"we set on for the coast at dawn":`'Set on' is not used for journeys; to start a trip, say 'set off' or 'set out'.`},wrong:`We set up for the coast at dawn.`,solutions:[`We set off for the coast at dawn.`,`We set out for the coast at dawn.`]},{id:`b2-ec-099`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'despite' without 'of', or 'in spite of' with 'of'. Example: Despite the cold, we swam in the lake.`,wrongReasons:{"despite of all the warnings, the climbers continued towards the summit":`'Despite' is never followed by 'of'; say 'despite' or 'in spite of'.`,"in spite all the warnings, the climbers continued towards the summit":`'In spite' needs 'of' after it: 'in spite of all the warnings'.`},wrong:`Despite of all the warnings, the climbers continued towards the summit.`,solutions:[`Despite all the warnings, the climbers continued towards the summit.`,`In spite of all the warnings, the climbers continued towards the summit.`]},{id:`b2-ec-100`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use only one linker to show contrast between two clauses: 'although' or 'but', not both. Example: Although it was late, we kept working.`,wrongReasons:{"although the tickets were expensive, but we bought them anyway":`'Although' and 'but' both show contrast, so using both in one sentence is incorrect. Keep only one.`,"although the tickets were expensive, however we bought them anyway":`'However' cannot join two clauses after 'although'; just remove the second linker.`},wrong:`Although the tickets were expensive, but we bought them anyway.`,solutions:[`Although the tickets were expensive, we bought them anyway.`,`The tickets were expensive, but we bought them anyway.`]},{id:`b2-ec-101`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Despite' is followed by a noun or -ing form, not by a full clause; before a clause use 'although'. Example: Despite feeling ill, he went to work.`,wrongReasons:{"despite she trained hard, she lost the final":`'Despite' cannot be followed by subject + verb. Use 'although she trained' or 'despite training'.`,"despite of she trained hard, she lost the final":`'Despite' never takes 'of', and it still cannot introduce a full clause.`},wrong:`Despite she trained hard, she lost the final.`,solutions:[`Although she trained hard, she lost the final.`,`Even though she trained hard, she lost the final.`,`Though she trained hard, she lost the final.`,`Despite training hard, she lost the final.`,`In spite of training hard, she lost the final.`,`Despite the fact that she trained hard, she lost the final.`,`In spite of the fact that she trained hard, she lost the final.`]},{id:`b2-ec-102`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Unless' already means 'if not', so the verb after it is positive. Example: We'll be late unless we hurry.`,wrongReasons:{"you won't pass the driving test unless you don't practise more":`'Unless' means 'if not', so 'unless you don't' is a double negative. Use 'unless you practise'.`,"you won't pass the driving test unless you will practise more":`After 'unless' we use the present simple, not 'will', even for the future.`},wrong:`You won't pass the driving test unless you don't practise more.`,solutions:[`You won't pass the driving test unless you practise more.`,`You will not pass the driving test unless you practise more.`,`You won't pass the driving test unless you practice more.`,`You will not pass the driving test unless you practice more.`,`You won't pass the driving test if you don't practise more.`,`You will not pass the driving test if you do not practise more.`,`You won't pass the driving test if you do not practise more.`,`You will not pass the driving test if you don't practise more.`,`You won't pass the driving test if you don't practice more.`,`You will not pass the driving test if you don't practice more.`]},{id:`b2-ec-103`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`After 'in case' we use the present simple to talk about a possible future situation. Example: Take some water in case you get thirsty.`,wrongReasons:{"take a jacket in case it will get cold later":`Do not use 'will' after 'in case'; use the present simple: 'in case it gets cold'.`,"take a jacket in case it would get cold later":`'Would' is not used after 'in case' with an imperative; use the present simple 'gets'.`},wrong:`Take a jacket in case it will get cold later.`,solutions:[`Take a jacket in case it gets cold later.`]},{id:`b2-ec-104`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Due to' and 'because of' are followed by a noun phrase; 'because', 'as' and 'since' are followed by a clause. Example: Because of the snow, the school closed.`,wrongReasons:{"due to the train was late, we missed the start of the film":`'Due to' must be followed by a noun, not by a subject and verb. Use 'because the train was late'.`,"because of the train was late, we missed the start of the film":`'Because of' also needs a noun phrase; before a full clause use just 'because'.`},wrong:`Due to the train was late, we missed the start of the film.`,solutions:[`Because the train was late, we missed the start of the film.`,`As the train was late, we missed the start of the film.`,`Since the train was late, we missed the start of the film.`,`Due to the late train, we missed the start of the film.`,`Because of the late train, we missed the start of the film.`,`Due to the train being late, we missed the start of the film.`,`Due to the fact that the train was late, we missed the start of the film.`]},{id:`b2-ec-105`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`When 'as well as' is followed by a verb, the verb takes the -ing form. Example: As well as singing, he plays the guitar.`,wrongReasons:{"as well as to manage the team, she handles the budget":`After 'as well as' at the start of a sentence, use the -ing form: 'as well as managing'.`,"as well as manage the team, she handles the budget":`The base form cannot follow 'as well as' here; use the -ing form 'managing'.`},wrong:`As well as to manage the team, she handles the budget.`,solutions:[`As well as managing the team, she handles the budget.`]},{id:`b2-ec-106`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The fixed linker for introducing a contrasting point is 'on the other hand'. Example: The flat is small. On the other hand, it is very cheap.`,wrongReasons:{"on the other side, the new job pays much better":`The fixed expression is 'on the other hand', not 'on the other side'.`,"in the other hand, the new job pays much better":`The preposition is 'on', not 'in': 'on the other hand'.`},wrong:`On the other side, the new job pays much better.`,solutions:[`On the other hand, the new job pays much better.`]},{id:`b2-ec-107`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'So that' is followed by a clause (subject + verb); 'so as to' and 'in order to' are followed by an infinitive. Example: She whispered so that nobody would hear.`,wrongReasons:{"we left early so that to avoid the traffic":`'So that' needs a subject and verb after it; with an infinitive use 'so as to' or 'in order to'.`,"we left early in order for avoid the traffic":`'In order for' needs a noun and 'to': simply use 'in order to avoid'.`},wrong:`We left early so that to avoid the traffic.`,solutions:[`We left early so as to avoid the traffic.`,`We left early in order to avoid the traffic.`,`We left early to avoid the traffic.`,`We left early so that we could avoid the traffic.`,`We left early so that we would avoid the traffic.`]},{id:`b2-ec-108`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The linker meaning 'except for' is 'apart from'. Example: Apart from Sam, everyone came to the party.`,wrongReasons:{"apart of the price, there was nothing wrong with the hotel":`The preposition after 'apart' is 'from', not 'of'.`,"a part from the price, there was nothing wrong with the hotel":`'Apart' is written as one word; 'a part' means a piece of something.`},wrong:`Apart of the price, there was nothing wrong with the hotel.`,solutions:[`Apart from the price, there was nothing wrong with the hotel.`,`Aside from the price, there was nothing wrong with the hotel.`]},{id:`b2-ec-109`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Neither' always pairs with 'nor', just as 'either' pairs with 'or'. Example: Neither Tom nor Ann was at home.`,wrongReasons:{"neither the manager or his assistant knew the answer":`'Neither' must be followed by 'nor', not 'or'.`,"neither the manager and his assistant knew the answer":`'And' cannot pair with 'neither'; the correct pair is 'neither ... nor'.`},wrong:`Neither the manager or his assistant knew the answer.`,solutions:[`Neither the manager nor his assistant knew the answer.`]},{id:`b2-ec-110`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Besides' (= in addition to) is a preposition and is followed directly by a noun or -ing form. Example: Besides teaching, she writes novels.`,wrongReasons:{"besides of being cheap, the flat is close to the station":`'Besides' is not followed by 'of'; say 'besides being cheap'.`,"besides to be cheap, the flat is close to the station":`After 'besides' use the -ing form, not the to-infinitive.`},wrong:`Besides of being cheap, the flat is close to the station.`,solutions:[`Besides being cheap, the flat is close to the station.`,`As well as being cheap, the flat is close to the station.`,`Apart from being cheap, the flat is close to the station.`,`In addition to being cheap, the flat is close to the station.`]},{id:`b2-ec-111`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'moreover' or 'furthermore' to add another positive point; 'nevertheless' introduces a contrast. Example: The car is fast. Moreover, it uses little fuel.`,wrongReasons:{"the hotel was in a perfect location. nevertheless, the staff were friendly and helpful":`'Nevertheless' shows contrast, but here the second point adds to the first. Use 'moreover' or 'in addition'.`,"the hotel was in a perfect location. however, the staff were friendly and helpful":`'However' also shows contrast; two positive points need an adding linker like 'moreover'.`},wrong:`The hotel was in a perfect location. Nevertheless, the staff were friendly and helpful.`,solutions:[`The hotel was in a perfect location. Moreover, the staff were friendly and helpful.`,`The hotel was in a perfect location. Furthermore, the staff were friendly and helpful.`,`The hotel was in a perfect location. In addition, the staff were friendly and helpful.`,`The hotel was in a perfect location. What is more, the staff were friendly and helpful.`,`The hotel was in a perfect location. What's more, the staff were friendly and helpful.`,`The hotel was in a perfect location. Also, the staff were friendly and helpful.`,`The hotel was in a perfect location. Besides, the staff were friendly and helpful.`]},{id:`b2-ec-112`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`In 'look forward to', 'to' is a preposition, so it is followed by the -ing form. Example: We look forward to hearing from you.`,wrongReasons:{"i'm looking forward to see you next week":`'To' in 'look forward to' is a preposition, so use the -ing form: 'to seeing'.`,"i'm looking forward see you next week":`'Look forward' needs 'to' and then an -ing form: 'look forward to seeing'.`},wrong:`I'm looking forward to see you next week.`,solutions:[`I'm looking forward to seeing you next week.`,`I am looking forward to seeing you next week.`]},{id:`b2-ec-113`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Avoid' is followed by the -ing form, not the to-infinitive. Example: She avoids driving at night.`,wrongReasons:{"he avoided to answer the journalist's question":`'Avoid' takes the -ing form: 'avoided answering'.`,"he avoided answer the journalist's question":`The base form cannot follow 'avoid'; use 'answering'.`},wrong:`He avoided to answer the journalist's question.`,solutions:[`He avoided answering the journalist's question.`]},{id:`b2-ec-114`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Decide' is followed by the to-infinitive. Example: We decided to stay at home.`,wrongReasons:{"they decided going to the coast for the weekend":`'Decide' takes the to-infinitive, not the -ing form: 'decided to go'.`,"they decided to going to the coast for the weekend":`After 'decided to', use the base form 'go', not 'going'.`},wrong:`They decided going to the coast for the weekend.`,solutions:[`They decided to go to the coast for the weekend.`]},{id:`b2-ec-115`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`In the active, 'make' + object is followed by the bare infinitive (without 'to'). Example: The film made me cry.`,wrongReasons:{"my parents made me to practise the piano every day":`After 'make someone', use the infinitive without 'to': 'made me practise'.`,"my parents made me practising the piano every day":`'Make' + object is not followed by the -ing form; use the bare infinitive 'practise'.`},wrong:`My parents made me to practise the piano every day.`,solutions:[`My parents made me practise the piano every day.`,`My parents made me practice the piano every day.`]},{id:`b2-ec-116`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Mind' is followed by the -ing form. Example: Do you mind waiting a moment?`,wrongReasons:{"would you mind to close the window":`'Mind' takes the -ing form: 'Would you mind closing...?'`,"would you mind close the window":`The base form cannot follow 'mind'; use 'closing'.`},wrong:`Would you mind to close the window?`,solutions:[`Would you mind closing the window?`]},{id:`b2-ec-117`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Remember to do' is for a future duty; 'remember doing' is a memory of the past. Example: Remember to post the letter.`,wrongReasons:{"please remember locking the door when you leave":`'Remember + -ing' refers to a memory of a past action. For something you must do later, use 'remember to lock'.`,"please remember lock the door when you leave":`'Remember' needs 'to' before the infinitive here: 'remember to lock'.`},wrong:`Please remember locking the door when you leave.`,solutions:[`Please remember to lock the door when you leave.`]},{id:`b2-ec-118`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Stop doing' means to finish a habit; 'stop to do' means to stop one activity in order to do another. Example: He stopped eating meat last year.`,wrongReasons:{"she stopped to smoke ten years ago and feels much healthier now":`'Stopped to smoke' means she paused in order to smoke. To say she quit the habit, use 'stopped smoking'.`,"she stopped smoke ten years ago and feels much healthier now":`'Stop' cannot be followed by the base form; use 'stopped smoking'.`},wrong:`She stopped to smoke ten years ago and feels much healthier now.`,solutions:[`She stopped smoking ten years ago and feels much healthier now.`]},{id:`b2-ec-119`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Advise' + object is followed by the to-infinitive; the negative is 'not to'. Example: She advised me not to wait.`,wrongReasons:{"the doctor advised him not eating so much salt":`After 'advise someone', use the to-infinitive: 'advised him not to eat'.`,"the doctor advised him to don't eat so much salt":`The negative infinitive is 'not to eat', not 'to don't eat'.`},wrong:`The doctor advised him not eating so much salt.`,solutions:[`The doctor advised him not to eat so much salt.`]},{id:`b2-ec-120`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Afford' is followed by the to-infinitive. Example: We can't afford to waste time.`,wrongReasons:{"i can't afford buying a new car this year":`'Afford' takes the to-infinitive, not the -ing form: 'afford to buy'.`,"i can't afford buy a new car this year":`You need 'to' before the verb: 'afford to buy'.`},wrong:`I can't afford buying a new car this year.`,solutions:[`I can't afford to buy a new car this year.`,`I cannot afford to buy a new car this year.`]},{id:`b2-ec-121`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The expression 'it's no use' is followed by the -ing form. Example: It's no use crying about it now.`,wrongReasons:{"it's no use to complain about the weather":`'It's no use' is followed by an -ing form: 'no use complaining'.`,"it's no use complain about the weather":`The base form cannot follow 'it's no use'; use 'complaining'.`},wrong:`It's no use to complain about the weather.`,solutions:[`It's no use complaining about the weather.`,`It is no use complaining about the weather.`]},{id:`b2-ec-122`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Let' + object is followed by the bare infinitive (without 'to'). Example: My boss let me go home early.`,wrongReasons:{"our teacher let us to leave early on friday":`After 'let someone', do not use 'to': 'let us leave'.`,"our teacher let us leaving early on friday":`'Let' + object is not followed by the -ing form; use the base form 'leave'.`},wrong:`Our teacher let us to leave early on Friday.`,solutions:[`Our teacher let us leave early on Friday.`]},{id:`b2-ec-123`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`In 'be used to' (= be accustomed to), 'to' is a preposition, so it is followed by the -ing form. Example: She is used to working late.`,wrongReasons:{"i'm used to get up at six now, so the early shift is easy for me":`'Be used to' means 'be accustomed to' and needs the -ing form: 'used to getting up'.`,"i'm use to get up at six now, so the early shift is easy for me":`The expression is 'be used to' with '-ed', followed by the -ing form 'getting'.`},wrong:`I'm used to get up at six now, so the early shift is easy for me.`,solutions:[`I'm used to getting up at six now, so the early shift is easy for me.`,`I am used to getting up at six now, so the early shift is easy for me.`]},{id:`b2-ec-124`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Admit' is followed by the -ing form (or 'to' + -ing), never by the to-infinitive. Example: She admitted breaking the vase.`,wrongReasons:{"the student admitted to copy his friend's homework":`'Admit' does not take the to-infinitive. Say 'admitted copying' or 'admitted to copying'.`,"the student admitted copy his friend's homework":`The base form cannot follow 'admit'; use 'copying'.`},wrong:`The student admitted to copy his friend's homework.`,solutions:[`The student admitted copying his friend's homework.`,`The student admitted to copying his friend's homework.`,`The student admitted having copied his friend's homework.`,`The student admitted to having copied his friend's homework.`,`The student admitted that he had copied his friend's homework.`,`The student admitted that he copied his friend's homework.`,`The student admitted he had copied his friend's homework.`,`The student admitted he copied his friend's homework.`]},{id:`b2-ec-125`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Consider' is followed by the -ing form. Example: Have you considered changing jobs?`,wrongReasons:{"we're considering to move to a smaller flat":`'Consider' takes the -ing form, not the to-infinitive: 'considering moving'.`,"we're considering about moving to a smaller flat":`'Consider' takes a direct object; do not add 'about'.`},wrong:`We're considering to move to a smaller flat.`,solutions:[`We're considering moving to a smaller flat.`,`We are considering moving to a smaller flat.`]},{id:`b2-ec-126`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use the past perfect for an action that happened before another past action. Example: When we got to the cinema, the film had started.`,wrongReasons:{"when i arrived at the station, the train already left":`The train left before I arrived, so use the past perfect: 'had already left'.`,"when i arrived at the station, the train has already left":`The present perfect does not fit a past story; use the past perfect 'had left'.`},wrong:`When I arrived at the station, the train already left.`,solutions:[`When I arrived at the station, the train had already left.`]},{id:`b2-ec-127`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for a longer background action and the past simple for a short action that interrupts it. Example: I was cooking when the lights went out.`,wrongReasons:{"i was having a shower when the phone was ringing":`The phone ringing interrupted the shower, so it needs the past simple: 'rang'.`,"i was having a shower when the phone has rung":`The present perfect cannot describe a single event in a past story; use 'rang'.`},wrong:`I was having a shower when the phone was ringing.`,solutions:[`I was having a shower when the phone rang.`]},{id:`b2-ec-128`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'By the time' + past simple is followed by the past perfect for the earlier action. Example: By the time I woke up, everyone had left.`,wrongReasons:{"by the time the firefighters arrived, the house burned down":`The house was destroyed before the firefighters arrived, so use the past perfect: 'had burned down'.`,"by the time the firefighters arrived, the house has burned down":`The present perfect does not fit a past narrative; use 'had burned down'.`},wrong:`By the time the firefighters arrived, the house burned down.`,solutions:[`By the time the firefighters arrived, the house had burned down.`,`By the time the firefighters arrived, the house had burnt down.`]},{id:`b2-ec-129`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`State verbs such as 'know', 'believe' and 'own' are not normally used in continuous tenses. Example: I knew her name.`,wrongReasons:{"he was knowing the answer, but he didn't say anything":`'Know' is a state verb, so it is not used in the past continuous. Use 'knew'.`,"he has known the answer, but he didn't say anything":`The present perfect does not match 'didn't say'; use the past simple 'knew'.`},wrong:`He was knowing the answer, but he didn't say anything.`,solutions:[`He knew the answer, but he didn't say anything.`,`He knew the answer, but he did not say anything.`]},{id:`b2-ec-130`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use the past perfect continuous for an activity that continued up to a moment in the past and explains a result. Example: He was tired because he had been running.`,wrongReasons:{"her eyes were red because she has been crying":`The story is in the past, so the earlier activity needs the past perfect continuous: 'had been crying'.`,"her eyes were red because she has cried":`The present perfect does not fit a past situation; use 'had been crying'.`},wrong:`Her eyes were red because she has been crying.`,solutions:[`Her eyes were red because she had been crying.`]},{id:`b2-ec-131`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use the past perfect for a change that happened before a past moment. Example: The town looked different because they had built a new road.`,wrongReasons:{"i didn't recognise my old classmate because he has changed so much":`The change happened before the meeting in the past, so use the past perfect: 'had changed'.`,"i didn't recognise my old classmate because he was changing so much":`The past continuous suggests the change was in progress at that moment; the change was already complete, so use 'had changed'.`},wrong:`I didn't recognise my old classmate because he has changed so much.`,solutions:[`I didn't recognise my old classmate because he had changed so much.`,`I did not recognise my old classmate because he had changed so much.`,`I didn't recognize my old classmate because he had changed so much.`,`I did not recognize my old classmate because he had changed so much.`]},{id:`b2-ec-132`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use the past simple for a single, complete event in the past. Example: Last week I found a wallet in the street.`,wrongReasons:{"yesterday i was meeting an old friend by chance in the supermarket":`Meeting someone by chance is a short, complete event, so use the past simple: 'met'.`,"yesterday i had met an old friend by chance in the supermarket":`The past perfect needs another past event to come after it; here a simple 'met' is correct.`},wrong:`Yesterday I was meeting an old friend by chance in the supermarket.`,solutions:[`Yesterday I met an old friend by chance in the supermarket.`]},{id:`b2-ec-133`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for an action that was in progress when something else happened. Example: We were watching TV when the storm began.`,wrongReasons:{"i slept when the earthquake started, so i didn't feel anything":`Sleeping was in progress when the earthquake started, so use the past continuous: 'was sleeping'.`,"i had slept when the earthquake started, so i didn't feel anything":`'Had slept' means the sleep was finished before the earthquake; use 'was sleeping'.`},wrong:`I slept when the earthquake started, so I didn't feel anything.`,solutions:[`I was sleeping when the earthquake started, so I didn't feel anything.`,`I was sleeping when the earthquake started, so I did not feel anything.`,`I was asleep when the earthquake started, so I didn't feel anything.`,`I was asleep when the earthquake started, so I did not feel anything.`]},{id:`b2-ec-134`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`After 'It was the first time', use the past perfect. Example: It was the first time she had seen snow.`,wrongReasons:{"it was the first time i ever flew in a plane":`'It was the first time' is followed by the past perfect: 'had ever flown'.`,"it was the first time i have ever flown in a plane":`With 'It was' (past), use the past perfect 'had flown', not the present perfect.`},wrong:`It was the first time I ever flew in a plane.`,solutions:[`It was the first time I had ever flown in a plane.`,`It was the first time I had ever flown on a plane.`]},{id:`b2-ec-135`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use the past simple for actions that happen one after another in a story; 'realise' is a state verb and is rarely continuous. Example: When he saw her face, he understood.`,wrongReasons:{"as soon as she opened the envelope, she was realising it was bad news":`Realising happened immediately after opening, as a single event, so use the past simple: 'realised'.`,"as soon as she opened the envelope, she had realised it was bad news":`The past perfect would mean she realised before opening it; use 'realised'.`},wrong:`As soon as she opened the envelope, she was realising it was bad news.`,solutions:[`As soon as she opened the envelope, she realised it was bad news.`,`As soon as she opened the envelope, she realized it was bad news.`]},{id:`b2-ec-136`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use the past simple for the single past event that comes after the past perfect action. Example: She had worked there for years when she retired.`,wrongReasons:{"we had lived in leeds for ten years when we were moving to bristol":`Moving was a single, complete event, so use the past simple: 'moved'.`,"we had lived in leeds for ten years when we have moved to bristol":`The present perfect cannot be used for a finished past event; use 'moved'.`},wrong:`We had lived in Leeds for ten years when we were moving to Bristol.`,solutions:[`We had lived in Leeds for ten years when we moved to Bristol.`]},{id:`b2-ec-137`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`In a past story, use the past simple (or past perfect) after 'before', not the present perfect. Example: We left before the rain started.`,wrongReasons:{"the thieves escaped before the police has arrived":`The present perfect does not fit a past story; use 'arrived' or 'had arrived'.`,"the thieves escaped before the police was arriving":`'Police' takes a plural verb, and a single arrival needs the past simple 'arrived'.`},wrong:`The thieves escaped before the police has arrived.`,solutions:[`The thieves escaped before the police arrived.`,`The thieves escaped before the police had arrived.`]},{id:`b2-ec-138`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Used to' for past habits is followed by the base form of the verb. Example: We used to live by the sea.`,wrongReasons:{"he used to playing tennis every saturday when he was young":`'Used to' (past habit) takes the base form: 'used to play'.`,"he use to playing tennis every saturday when he was young":`The past habit form is 'used to' + base form: 'used to play'.`},wrong:`He used to playing tennis every Saturday when he was young.`,solutions:[`He used to play tennis every Saturday when he was young.`,`He would play tennis every Saturday when he was young.`,`He played tennis every Saturday when he was young.`]},{id:`b2-ec-139`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`In time clauses after 'when', 'as soon as' or 'before', use the present simple for the future. Example: I'll call you when I get home.`,wrongReasons:{"i will tell her when i will see her tomorrow":`Do not use 'will' after 'when' in a time clause; use the present simple: 'when I see her'.`,"i will tell her when i would see her tomorrow":`'Would' is also wrong in a future time clause; use the present simple 'see'.`},wrong:`I will tell her when I will see her tomorrow.`,solutions:[`I will tell her when I see her tomorrow.`,`I'll tell her when I see her tomorrow.`]},{id:`b2-ec-140`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use the future continuous for an action that will be in progress at a specific time in the future. Example: At nine tomorrow, I'll be taking my exam.`,wrongReasons:{"this time next week i will lie on a beach in greece":`'This time next week' needs an action in progress, so use the future continuous: 'will be lying'.`,"this time next week i will lying on a beach in greece":`The future continuous needs 'be': 'will be lying'.`},wrong:`This time next week I will lie on a beach in Greece.`,solutions:[`This time next week I will be lying on a beach in Greece.`,`This time next week I'll be lying on a beach in Greece.`]},{id:`b2-ec-141`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Be going to' is followed by the base form of the verb. Example: She's going to start a new course.`,wrongReasons:{"i'm going to visiting my cousin in madrid next month":`After 'going to' for a plan, use the base form: 'going to visit'.`,"i'm going visit my cousin in madrid next month":`You need 'to' between 'going' and the verb: 'going to visit'.`},wrong:`I'm going to visiting my cousin in Madrid next month.`,solutions:[`I'm going to visit my cousin in Madrid next month.`,`I am going to visit my cousin in Madrid next month.`,`I'm visiting my cousin in Madrid next month.`,`I am visiting my cousin in Madrid next month.`]},{id:`b2-ec-142`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`After 'when' in a future time clause, use the present simple or present perfect, not 'will'. Example: We'll start when everyone arrives.`,wrongReasons:{"shall we go out when the rain will stop":`'Will' is not used after 'when' in a time clause; say 'when the rain stops'.`,"shall we go out when the rain stopped":`The past simple does not refer to the future here; use 'stops'.`},wrong:`Shall we go out when the rain will stop?`,solutions:[`Shall we go out when the rain stops?`,`Shall we go out when the rain has stopped?`]},{id:`b2-ec-143`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`After 'until' we use the present simple to refer to the future. Example: Stay here until I come back.`,wrongReasons:{"we'll wait here until the taxi will come":`Do not use 'will' after 'until'; use the present simple: 'until the taxi comes'.`,"we'll wait here until the taxi is coming":`The present continuous is not used after 'until' for a single future event; use 'comes'.`},wrong:`We'll wait here until the taxi will come.`,solutions:[`We'll wait here until the taxi comes.`,`We will wait here until the taxi comes.`]},{id:`b2-ec-144`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The future perfect active is 'will have' + past participle, for an action complete before a future time. Example: By Friday, I will have finished the report.`,wrongReasons:{"by the time you get here, i will have been cooked dinner":`'Will have been cooked' is passive, but 'I' did the cooking; use the active 'will have cooked'.`,"by the time you get here, i will be cooked dinner":`'Will be cooked' is passive and does not show completion; use 'will have cooked'.`},wrong:`By the time you get here, I will have been cooked dinner.`,solutions:[`By the time you get here, I will have cooked dinner.`,`By the time you get here, I'll have cooked dinner.`]},{id:`b2-ec-145`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use the future perfect (continuous) to say how long an action will have lasted at a future point. Example: Next year we will have been living here for a decade.`,wrongReasons:{"in june, i will be working for this company for twenty years":`With 'for twenty years' up to a future point, use the future perfect continuous: 'will have been working'.`,"in june, i will work for this company for twenty years":`'Will work' does not show duration up to a point; use 'will have been working'.`},wrong:`In June, I will be working for this company for twenty years.`,solutions:[`In June, I will have been working for this company for twenty years.`,`In June, I'll have been working for this company for twenty years.`,`In June, I will have worked for this company for twenty years.`,`In June, I'll have worked for this company for twenty years.`]},{id:`b2-ec-146`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Be about to' (= going to happen very soon) is followed by the base form. Example: Hurry, the show is about to begin.`,wrongReasons:{"the conference is about to starting, so please take your seats":`'Be about to' takes the base form: 'is about to start'.`,"the conference is about starting, so please take your seats":`The structure needs 'to' + base form: 'about to start'.`},wrong:`The conference is about to starting, so please take your seats.`,solutions:[`The conference is about to start, so please take your seats.`]},{id:`b2-ec-147`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Use 'will' for promises and offers. Example: I'll help you with your bags.`,wrongReasons:{"i promise i call you as soon as i land":`A promise about the future needs 'will': 'I promise I'll call you'.`,"i promise i am calling you as soon as i land":`The present continuous is for arrangements, not promises; use 'will call'.`},wrong:`I promise I call you as soon as I land.`,solutions:[`I promise I will call you as soon as I land.`,`I promise I'll call you as soon as I land.`,`I promise to call you as soon as I land.`]},{id:`b2-ec-148`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Be due to' + base form is used for something planned or expected at a certain time. Example: The plane is due to land at five.`,wrongReasons:{"the new hospital is due opening in the spring":`'Be due' is followed by the to-infinitive: 'is due to open'.`,"the new hospital is due open in the spring":`You need 'to' after 'due': 'due to open'.`},wrong:`The new hospital is due opening in the spring.`,solutions:[`The new hospital is due to open in the spring.`]},{id:`b2-ec-149`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Will' is a modal verb and is followed by the base form without 'to'. Example: The shop will close soon.`,wrongReasons:{"hurry up! the bus will to leave in two minutes":`'Will' is never followed by 'to'; say 'will leave'.`,"hurry up! the bus will leaving in two minutes":`After 'will', use the base form 'leave', not the -ing form.`},wrong:`Hurry up! The bus will to leave in two minutes.`,solutions:[`Hurry up! The bus will leave in two minutes.`,`Hurry up! The bus leaves in two minutes.`,`Hurry up! The bus is leaving in two minutes.`,`Hurry up! The bus is going to leave in two minutes.`]},{id:`b2-ec-150`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The future continuous is 'will be' + -ing and is often used to ask politely about plans. Example: Will you be staying for dinner?`,wrongReasons:{"will you be use the car this evening? i need to go to the gym":`After 'will be' you need the -ing form: 'will you be using'.`,"will you be used the car this evening? i need to go to the gym":`'Will be used' is passive; to ask about someone's plans, use 'will be using'.`},wrong:`Will you be use the car this evening? I need to go to the gym.`,solutions:[`Will you be using the car this evening? I need to go to the gym.`,`Will you use the car this evening? I need to go to the gym.`,`Are you using the car this evening? I need to go to the gym.`,`Are you going to use the car this evening? I need to go to the gym.`]},{id:`b2-ec-151`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`For predictions, use 'will' or 'be going to'; the present continuous is for personal arrangements. Example: Experts say prices will rise next year.`,wrongReasons:{"the forecast says it is snowing tomorrow":`The weather is a prediction, not an arrangement, so use 'will snow' or 'is going to snow'.`,"the forecast says it snows tomorrow":`The present simple is for timetables, not weather predictions; use 'will snow'.`},wrong:`The forecast says it is snowing tomorrow.`,solutions:[`The forecast says it will snow tomorrow.`,`The forecast says it'll snow tomorrow.`,`The forecast says it is going to snow tomorrow.`,`The forecast says it's going to snow tomorrow.`,`The forecast says it will be snowing tomorrow.`,`The forecast says it'll be snowing tomorrow.`]},{id:`b2-ec-152`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The collocation is 'do someone a favour'. Example: Could you do me a favour and feed the cat?`,wrongReasons:{"can you make me a favour and carry this box":`We 'do' a favour, not 'make' it.`,"can you give me a favour and carry this box":`'Give' does not collocate with 'favour' here; the fixed phrase is 'do me a favour'.`},wrong:`Can you make me a favour and carry this box?`,solutions:[`Can you do me a favour and carry this box?`,`Can you do me a favor and carry this box?`]},{id:`b2-ec-153`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The collocation is 'make a phone call'. Example: She made three calls this morning.`,wrongReasons:{"i have to do a phone call before the meeting starts":`We 'make' a phone call, not 'do' one.`,"i have to give a phone call before the meeting starts":`In standard English we 'make' a phone call; 'give a call' is only used in informal phrases like 'give me a call'.`},wrong:`I have to do a phone call before the meeting starts.`,solutions:[`I have to make a phone call before the meeting starts.`]},{id:`b2-ec-154`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The collocation is 'make a mistake'. Example: Everyone makes mistakes when learning.`,wrongReasons:{"the new cashier did two mistakes on his first day":`We 'make' mistakes, not 'do' them.`,"the new cashier took two mistakes on his first day":`'Take' does not collocate with 'mistake'; use 'made'.`},wrong:`The new cashier did two mistakes on his first day.`,solutions:[`The new cashier made two mistakes on his first day.`]},{id:`b2-ec-155`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Rise' (rose, risen) has no object; 'raise' (raised) needs an object. Example: Prices rose, so the company raised its salaries.`,wrongReasons:{"the price of petrol has raised sharply this year":`'Raise' needs an object (someone raises prices). Prices go up by themselves, so use 'has risen'.`,"the price of petrol has rose sharply this year":`'Rose' is the past simple; after 'has' you need the past participle 'risen'.`},wrong:`The price of petrol has raised sharply this year.`,solutions:[`The price of petrol has risen sharply this year.`,`The price of petrol has gone up sharply this year.`]},{id:`b2-ec-156`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`You 'lend' something to someone; you 'borrow' something from someone. Example: Can I borrow your pen? / Can you lend me your pen?`,wrongReasons:{"could you borrow me your umbrella":`'Borrow' means to take something from someone; when you ask someone to give it, use 'lend'.`,"could you borrow to me your umbrella":`Adding 'to' does not fix it: 'borrow' means take, so use 'lend me'.`},wrong:`Could you borrow me your umbrella?`,solutions:[`Could you lend me your umbrella?`,`Could I borrow your umbrella?`]},{id:`b2-ec-157`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`Adjectives in -ing describe what causes a feeling; -ed adjectives describe how a person feels. Example: The lecture was boring, so I was bored.`,wrongReasons:{"the film was so bored that i fell asleep":`The film causes the feeling, so use the -ing adjective: 'boring'.`,"the film was so boredom that i fell asleep":`'Boredom' is a noun; after 'so' you need the adjective 'boring'.`},wrong:`The film was so bored that I fell asleep.`,solutions:[`The film was so boring that I fell asleep.`]},{id:`b2-ec-158`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Affect' is a verb with a direct object; 'effect' is a noun used in 'have an effect on'. Example: Stress affects sleep. / Stress has an effect on sleep.`,wrongReasons:{"lack of sleep can affect on your memory":`'Affect' takes a direct object with no preposition: 'can affect your memory'.`,"lack of sleep can effect on your memory":`'Effect' is normally a noun; use the verb 'affect' or 'have an effect on'.`},wrong:`Lack of sleep can affect on your memory.`,solutions:[`Lack of sleep can affect your memory.`,`Lack of sleep can have an effect on your memory.`]},{id:`b2-ec-159`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Sensitive' means easily upset or affected by feelings; 'sensible' means practical and reasonable. Example: He's sensitive about his age.`,wrongReasons:{"she is very sensible and cries at sad films":`'Sensible' means practical and wise; a person who cries easily is 'sensitive'.`,"she is very sensitivity and cries at sad films":`'Sensitivity' is a noun; after 'very' you need the adjective 'sensitive'.`},wrong:`She is very sensible and cries at sad films.`,solutions:[`She is very sensitive and cries at sad films.`]},{id:`b2-ec-160`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Research' is uncountable (no 'a/an') and collocates with 'do', 'carry out' or 'conduct'. Example: They did research into sleep.`,wrongReasons:{"the scientist made an important research on climate":`'Research' is uncountable, so it cannot take 'an', and we 'do' or 'carry out' research rather than 'make' it.`,"the scientist made important researches on climate":`'Research' has no plural form in standard use, and the verb should be 'did' or 'carried out'.`},wrong:`The scientist made an important research on climate.`,solutions:[`The scientist did important research on climate.`,`The scientist carried out important research on climate.`,`The scientist conducted important research on climate.`,`The scientist did some important research on climate.`]},{id:`b2-ec-161`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Remind someone to do something' means to help them not forget; 'remember' has no person object here. Example: Remind me to call Mum.`,wrongReasons:{"can you remember me to buy some bread on the way home":`When you ask another person to make you remember, use 'remind me', not 'remember me'.`,"can you remember to me to buy some bread on the way home":`Adding 'to' does not work; the verb with a person object is 'remind'.`},wrong:`Can you remember me to buy some bread on the way home?`,solutions:[`Can you remind me to buy some bread on the way home?`]},{id:`b2-ec-162`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`'Travel' is usually an uncountable noun for the general activity; for one visit somewhere, use 'trip' or 'journey'. Example: Our trip to Rome was wonderful.`,wrongReasons:{"our travel to peru was the best holiday of my life":`'Travel' describes travelling in general; for one particular visit, say 'our trip'.`,"our travelling to peru was the best holiday of my life":`'Travelling' is also the general activity; a single holiday visit is a 'trip'.`},wrong:`Our travel to Peru was the best holiday of my life.`,solutions:[`Our trip to Peru was the best holiday of my life.`,`Our journey to Peru was the best holiday of my life.`]},{id:`b2-ec-163`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The collocation is 'pay attention to'. Example: Please pay attention to the safety rules.`,wrongReasons:{"the teacher asked us to put attention to the instructions":`We 'pay' attention, not 'put' it.`,"the teacher asked us to make attention to the instructions":`'Make' does not collocate with 'attention'; use 'pay attention'.`},wrong:`The teacher asked us to put attention to the instructions.`,solutions:[`The teacher asked us to pay attention to the instructions.`]},{id:`b2-ec-164`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`The collocation is 'break a record'; you 'beat' a person or team. Example: She broke the national record.`,wrongReasons:{"he won the race and beat the world record":`We 'break' a record; 'beat' is used for opponents.`,"he won the race and won the world record":`You cannot 'win' a record; the collocation is 'break a record'.`},wrong:`He won the race and beat the world record.`,solutions:[`He won the race and broke the world record.`]},{id:`b2-ec-165`,type:`error-correct`,level:`B2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This sentence has a mistake. Rewrite it correctly.`,explanation:`An 'employer' gives people jobs; an 'employee' works for an employer. Example: The bank has 300 employees.`,wrongReasons:{"the company has hired fifty new employers this year":`'Employers' are the people or companies who hire; the people hired are 'employees'.`,"the company has hired fifty new employes this year":`The spelling is 'employees', with a double 'e'.`},wrong:`The company has hired fifty new employers this year.`,solutions:[`The company has hired fifty new employees this year.`,`The company has hired fifty new members of staff this year.`,`The company has hired fifty new workers this year.`,`The company has hired fifty new staff this year.`]}]};export{e as default};